WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.381
Updating a runtime or changing a model can affect behavior, performance, compatibility, and support expectations.

2
00:00:07.381 --> 00:00:10.586
A new runtime version may change service behavior.

3
00:00:10.586 --> 00:00:16.550
A new model tag may change response style, memory needs, latency, or application assumptions.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:22.589
A configuration change may alter storage paths, network exposure, or how a model is kept loaded.

5
00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:31.311
Teams should document known-good combinations of runtime version, model version, hardware, configuration, and integration behavior.

6
00:00:31.311 --> 00:00:33.772
This does not need to be a heavy process.

7
00:00:33.772 --> 00:00:44.507
It can be a short operational note that records what was tested, who approved shared use, what workflows depend on it, and what rollback option exists if users see a problem.

8
00:00:44.507 --> 00:00:48.309
Backups should focus on the assets that matter for recovery.

9
00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:54.720
Model files can often be re-pulled, but undocumented configuration decisions are harder to recover.

10
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:07.245
Modelfiles, service configuration notes, approved model inventories, integration settings, routing assumptions, and support documentation may be more important than a copy of every model file.

11
00:01:07.245 --> 00:01:11.792
Rollback planning matters when shared tools depend on the environment.

12
00:01:11.792 --> 00:01:22.379
If a runtime update causes errors or a model change alters behavior, operators should know how to restore the previous known-good state or route users to an alternative.

13
00:01:22.379 --> 00:01:26.106
Change control is not about slowing down experimentation.

14
00:01:26.106 --> 00:01:34.530
It is about making operational changes clear enough that teams know what changed, why it changed, and how to respond if it does not work.
